---The firewall policy defines the behavior of a firewall using a collection of stateless and stateful rule groups and other settings. You can use one firewall policy for multiple firewalls.
---@class aws.network_firewall.FirewallPolicy
---@field StatelessRuleGroupReferences? aws.network_firewall.StatelessRuleGroupReferences References to the stateless rule groups that are used in the policy. These define the matching criteria in stateless rules.
---@field StatelessDefaultActions aws.network_firewall.StatelessActions The actions to take on a packet if it doesn't match any of the stateless rules in the policy. If you want non-matching packets to be forwarded for stateful inspection, specify aws:forward_to_sfe.
---@field StatelessFragmentDefaultActions aws.network_firewall.StatelessActions The actions to take on a fragmented UDP packet if it doesn't match any of the stateless rules in the policy. Network Firewall only manages UDP packet fragments and silently drops packet fragments for other protocols. If you want non-matching fragmented UDP packets to be forwarded for stateful inspection, specify aws:forward_to_sfe.
---@field StatelessCustomActions? aws.network_firewall.CustomActions The custom action definitions that are available for use in the firewall policy's StatelessDefaultActions setting. You name each custom action that you define, and then you can use it by name in your default actions specifications.
---@field StatefulRuleGroupReferences? aws.network_firewall.StatefulRuleGroupReferences References to the stateful rule groups that are used in the policy. These define the inspection criteria in stateful rules.
---@field StatefulDefaultActions? aws.network_firewall.StatefulActions The default actions to take on a packet that doesn't match any stateful rules. The stateful default action is optional, and is only valid when using the strict rule order.
---@field StatefulEngineOptions? aws.network_firewall.StatefulEngineOptions Additional options governing how Network Firewall handles stateful rules. The stateful rule groups that you use in your policy must have stateful rule options settings that are compatible with these settings.
---@field TLSInspectionConfigurationArn? aws.network_firewall.ResourceArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the TLS inspection configuration.
---@field PolicyVariables? aws.network_firewall.PolicyVariables Contains variables that you can use to override default Suricata settings in your firewall policy.
---@field EnableTLSSessionHolding? aws.network_firewall.EnableTLSSessionHolding When true, prevents TCP and TLS packets from reaching destination servers until TLS Inspection has evaluated Server Name Indication (SNI) rules. Requires an associated TLS Inspection configuration.
---@field Route53ResolvedIPValidation? aws.network_firewall.Route53ResolvedIPValidation The settings that enable resolved IP validation for the policy. Resolved IP validation verifies that a connection's destination IP address is one that the Amazon Route 53 Resolver returned in a DNS response for a domain. A VPC that's associated with the Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule group that you specify must send the response. This checks how the destination IP address was obtained, instea...

