---A single structured firewall engine rule, for use in a firewall engine rule group or as an embedded rule in a Next Generation policy. A firewall rule accepts, drops, or rejects traffic at a connection-state hook. For more information about authoring firewall engine rules, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
---@class aws.network_firewall.FirewallRule
---@field Action aws.network_firewall.FirewallRuleAction|string The action that the firewall engine takes on traffic that matches the rule. The firewall engine supports the following actions:
---@field ActionScope aws.network_firewall.ActionScope|string The scope that controls how much of the matched traffic the action applies to. The valid scopes depend on the action: ACCEPT supports all four scopes, and DROP, REJECT, REJECT_DST, and REJECT_BOTH support only PACKET and FLOW.
---@field Hook aws.network_firewall.HookKey|string The connection-state hook at which the firewall engine evaluates the rule. The hook encodes both the protocol and the connection state, for example a TCP hook or a TLS client_hello hook. For the hooks that the firewall engine supports and how they map to the connection lifecycle, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide and the Suricata ...
---@field Protocol aws.network_firewall.FirewallRuleProtocol|string The protocol of the rule's hook. The protocol is derived from the hook and must match the hook's protocol prefix. Network Firewall rejects a rule whose protocol doesn't match its hook.
---@field SourceIPs? aws.network_firewall.Source The source IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule, so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source and destination addresses.
---@field DestIPs? aws.network_firewall.Destination The destination IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule, so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source and destination addresses.
---@field SourcePorts? aws.network_firewall.Port The source ports to inspect for.
---@field DestPorts? aws.network_firewall.Port The destination ports to inspect for.
---@field RuleOptions? aws.network_firewall.RuleOptions Additional options for the rule. These are the Suricata RuleOptions settings, such as the signature ID (sid) and match keywords.
---@field AcceptPriorHooks? aws.network_firewall.AcceptPriorHooks Whether Network Firewall automatically accepts the earlier application-layer hooks that a connection must pass to reach this rule's hook. Because the firewall engine is drop by default, accepting prior hooks lets you author a single rule for the hook you care about, instead of also authoring a rule for each earlier hook. This setting accepts the earlier application-layer hooks only; it doesn't acc...
---@field AlertEnabled? aws.network_firewall.AlertEnabled Specifies whether the rule also generates an alert log for the traffic that it matches. Turning on alerting adds a secondary alert action to the rule, so that Network Firewall logs the match in addition to taking the rule's primary action.
---@field BypassIPSEnabled? aws.network_firewall.BypassIPSEnabled Specifies whether an accepted connection bypasses the IPS engine. By default, traffic that the firewall engine accepts advances to the IPS engine for threat inspection. When you turn on this setting for an accept rule, the accepted traffic goes straight to its destination without IPS inspection. You can set the bypass only on accept rules, and it uses the same scope as the rule's accept action; on...
---@field RuleMetadata? aws.network_firewall.RuleMetadata Optional metadata for the rule: whether the rule is enabled and a description. Metadata doesn't change how the rule matches or acts on traffic.

---@alias aws.network_firewall.FirewallRuleAction "ACCEPT"|"DROP"|"REJECT"|"REJECT_DST"|"REJECT_BOTH"

---The object that defines a firewall engine rule group, for use in a Next Generation policy. A firewall engine rule group provides access-control rules that the firewall engine evaluates at connection-state hooks. You provide the rules as a Suricata-compatible rule string, as structured firewall rules, or generated from a list of domain names. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall usi...
---@class aws.network_firewall.FirewallRuleGroup
---@field RulesSource aws.network_firewall.FirewallRulesSource The stateful inspection criteria for the firewall engine rule group.
